<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-inverse-laplace-pfd-linear" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Inverse Laplace with Distinct Linear Factors</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       TABLE REFERENCE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Keep the Laplace transform table handy as a reference for this page.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-laplace-ref-4-2-1">
    <title>Laplace Transform Table</title>
    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>F(s) = \mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}</m></cell>
        <cell>Condition</cell>
        <cell>Formula</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{s}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>A</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{s-a}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>B</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>t^n</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>C</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\sin(at)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{a}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>D</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\cos(at)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{s}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>E</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>t^n e^{at}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>H</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}\sin(bt)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{b}{(s-a)^2 + b^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>I</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}\cos(bt)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2 + b^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>J</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       INTRO
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Sometimes the <m>F(s)</m> we want to invert doesn't match any row in the table directly.
    When the denominator is a product of factors, we can use partial fraction decomposition
    to break it into simpler pieces that do match the table.
    This technique will come up a lot in the next section when we actually use the Laplace
    transform to solve differential equations.
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: L^{-1}{1/((s-2)(s+4))}
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Find <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{1}{(s-2)(s+4)}\right\}</m>.
    Notice this expression does not appear anywhere in the table. On the previous page
    we worked out the partial fraction decomposition (see <xref ref="ex-pf-clear-denominators"/>):
    <me>\frac{1}{(s-2)(s+4)} = \frac{\frac{1}{6}}{s-2} - \frac{\frac{1}{6}}{s+4}.</me>
    Each fraction now matches <xref ref="formula-B" text="custom">Formula B</xref>,
    <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}\} = \dfrac{1}{s-a}</m>. Applying the inverse transform:
    <md>
      <mrow>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1}{(s-2)(s+4)}\right\}
        \amp=\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{\frac{1}{6}}{s-2} - \frac{\frac{1}{6}}{s+4}\right\}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{
              \underbrace{\frac{\frac{1}{6}}{s-2}}_{\text{Formula B}, \, a=2}
            \right\}
            - \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{
              \underbrace{\frac{\frac{1}{6}}{s+4}}_{\text{Formula B}, \, a=-4}
            \right\}</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{6}e^{2t} - \frac{1}{6}e^{-4t}.</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          When <m>F(s)</m> doesn't match the table directly, use partial fraction decomposition
          to break it into pieces that do.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          After decomposing, apply linearity and match each piece to a formula in the table.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          Distinct linear factors in the denominator each give a term matching
          <xref ref="formula-B" text="custom">Formula B</xref>.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

